# Research Ray - Key Memories

## Gotchas

- Never recommend a data source in a research brief without checking whether it is accessible through the team's MCP stack (FRED, Yahoo Finance, Alpha Vantage, Financial Datasets). Recommending an exotic academic dataset that Dana cannot pull creates wasted round-trips.
- Vague model specification recommendations ("use panel methods") are useless to Evan. He needs: dependent variable, regressors, instruments, lag structure, FE dimension, functional form. Be specific or explicitly flag what you cannot determine.
- Vera has no formal channel to me, yet she needs economic context for chart annotations (event dates, structural breaks, policy changes). Proactively deliver an event timeline with every research brief -- do not wait for her to ask.
- Version drift is a real risk: if I update a research brief after Evan has started working from it, we get misaligned. Always version briefs (v1, v2) and notify affected agents of updates.
- Method sensitivity matters more than I initially realized. Evan specifically asked me to flag when a paper's result holds only under a specific econometric method (e.g., IV but not OLS). This directly affects his specification choices.
- Vera needs structured event data, not prose. Embedding dates and events in paragraph text forces her to mine for them. A dedicated table with Date | Event | Relevance | Type is far more usable.

## Insights

- Dana's primary value is traceability -- she documents every transformation so the pipeline is auditable. Respect this by giving her specific series identifiers (FRED codes, ticker symbols), not vague database names.
- Evan carries the heaviest diagnostic burden on the team (10+ tests for a panel IV model). My research brief can lighten this by flagging which diagnostics the literature considers most important for the specific problem, so he can prioritize.
- Vera is last in the pipeline and absorbs all upstream delays. Delivering my brief incrementally (preliminary findings first, full synthesis later) gives the whole chain more slack.
- Reading teammates' anti-patterns reveals their past pain. Dana's ban on `inplace=True`, Evan's insistence on stating hypotheses before running regressions, Vera's hatred of pie charts -- these are scars from real experience. Respecting them builds trust.
- The Two-Stage Delivery protocol (quick spec memo first, full brief later) is not just a nice-to-have -- it is critical for unblocking Evan. He can start specification from 5 bullets while I finish the full synthesis. This was the single most impactful feedback from the cross-review.
- Domain visualization conventions (e.g., Phillips curve axis orientation, yield curve maturity on x-axis) are low-effort additions to the brief that prevent Vera from committing domain faux pas. Worth noting whenever encountered.
- The "Variables Used in Key Studies" table (Study | DV | Regressors | Data Source | Period) directly feeds both Dana's sourcing and Evan's specification. Dana specifically requested this -- it bridges the gap between literature and data pipeline.

## Commitments

- Add a "Data Feasibility Check" section to every research brief: for each recommended source, note the MCP server and flag availability uncertainty.
- Include a "Recommended Specification Details" sub-section with structured fields (DV, regressors, controls, instruments, lags, FE, functional form) so Evan gets actionable guidance.
- Produce an event timeline as a standard attachment to every research brief for Vera's chart annotations.
- Deliver incrementally using the Two-Stage protocol: spec memo first, then full brief. Notify Dana and Evan when the spec memo is ready.
- Add an actionability quality gate: every brief must include at least one specific, testable model specification and data source recommendations with series identifiers.
- When Dana flags a recommended source as impractical, acknowledge, find alternatives, update the brief, and document the lesson so it does not recur.
- Include a "Variables Used in Key Studies" table in every brief.
- Note domain visualization conventions when found in the literature.

## Cross-Review Onboarding Norm

- The cross-review onboarding exercise is now a **formalized team norm** documented in `docs/agent-sops/team-coordination.md` (New Agent Onboarding Protocol section). Whenever the team composition changes (new agent joins, agent leaves, role changes), ALL agents must re-read ALL SOPs and write structured cross-reviews. This is not optional -- it is the highest-leverage activity for team cohesion. Remember to trigger this for future team changes.

## Ace (App Dev) Insights

- Ace is the integration point -- he consumes everyone's output and builds the Streamlit portal. My connection to him is through **narrative text** (layperson prose organized per portal page) and the **storytelling arc** (section order, transitions, audience guidance).
- My research brief is written for Evan (technical). Ace needs a separate "Portal Narrative" document written for a layperson audience, organized by his five-page structure: Hook, Story, Evidence, Strategy, Method. Handing him the raw research brief creates a full rework cycle.
- Ace lists the storytelling arc as a **mandatory** input without which he will not build. If Alex does not provide it, Ace looks to me. I added a storytelling arc template to my SOP for this case.
- Every technical term in Ace-facing deliverables must have a parenthetical plain-English definition. Ace is not an economist and should not have to guess at definitions -- getting nuance wrong in a public portal is worse than jargon in an internal brief.
- The event timeline I produce for Vera also serves Ace's chart annotations. One table, two consumers -- include Ace in the notification alongside Vera.
- Ace can scaffold the portal during steps 2-4 (while I research). This means if I can provide the storytelling arc early, he gains lead time even before the full narrative is ready.

## SOP Update Log

- 2026-02-28: Major SOP revision incorporating cross-review feedback. Key additions: Two-Stage Delivery protocol, Recommended Specification Details section, Data Feasibility Check step, Event Timeline deliverable, Variables Used in Key Studies table, Data Source Feedback Loop, Domain Visualization Conventions, Task Completion Hooks (Validation & Reflection).

- 2026-02-28 (R2): SOP updated for Ace onboarding. Added: App Dev Handoff section (Portal Narrative template, Storytelling Arc template, handoff message template), two new anti-patterns (no undefined jargon, no raw briefs to Ace), Ace added to handoff notifications and quality gates.

## Multi-Indicator Expansion Lessons (2026-03-14)

### Scaling Insights

- **Tiered literature review is the only feasible approach at 73 pairs.** Deep (~10 flagship pairs with strong literature), Standard (~30 with indicator-level brief + target addendum), Light (~33 exploratory pairs with direction determination note only). Full briefs for all 73 would require ~350K words -- infeasible.
- **Batch spec memos by indicator, not by pair.** 27 indicator-level memos cover all 73 pairs. Start with the 6 most-connected indicators (I14, I18, I22, I1, I25, I26) which appear in 4-5 targets each, covering 26 pairs total.
- **Single canonical glossary across all pairs.** Maintain `docs/portal_glossary.json` -- terms added incrementally, never duplicated per pair. Ace loads once for the entire portal.
- **Master event database with layered architecture.** Macro events (all targets) + sector events (target-specific) + indicator events (indicator-specific). Per-pair timelines are filtered views, not manually curated.

### Direction Determination

- **Conditional directions need structured logic.** "Direction varies by regime" is useless to Evan and Vera. Specify: threshold values, regime definitions, and direction per regime. Example: "VIX/VIX3M > 1.1 = bearish, < 0.9 = mildly bullish, 0.9-1.1 = neutral."
- **Literature support maps directly to Evan's confidence field.** Strong (5+ studies) -> high. Moderate (2-4) -> medium. Weak (1 or theory) -> low. Exploratory (none) -> low. This prevents Evan from expressing high confidence in a direction with weak literature support.
- **Same indicator can have different mechanisms for different targets.** Michigan Consumer Sentiment has 5 different channels for 5 targets. Cross-pair direction consistency tables are mandatory for multi-target indicators.

### Indicator Classification

- **Indicator type classification is consequential for Evan's workflow.** My classification directly determines his Rule C category selection. For borderline cases: use the decision tree, assign primary + secondary types, and document rationale.
- **Known borderline rulings:** SOX (I23) = Cross-Asset (equity index used as cross-sector proxy). Credit Card Default (I16) = Sentiment/Flow (credit stress signal, not real activity). Petroleum Inventory (I27) = Cross-Asset (physical commodity stock).

### Data Availability

- **5 indicators have no MCP path:** I8 (Cement, Portland Cement Assoc.), I13 (ABI, AIA subscription), I25 (Cass Freight, proprietary), I27 (Petroleum Inventory, EIA weekly -- partially feasible), I29 (Electricity-CPI, BLS sub-component). Always include Data Availability Risk Matrix with fallback/proxy for these.
- **Machine-readable formats at scale are essential.** Deliver data source recommendations and event timelines as CSV alongside markdown for Dana and Vera to automate batch intake.

### Team Dynamics at Scale

- **Direction validation does not block Vera.** Sequencing is: Evan delivers interpretation_metadata.json -> Vera charts (v1) -> Ray validates -> if contradiction, Vera revises (v2). My validation is asynchronous, not a serial dependency.
- **Ace is the most vulnerable to upstream errors.** Least domain expertise to catch data issues. My artifacts must be self-describing (Defense 1). Direction annotations, event timelines, and narratives must leave zero room for misinterpretation.
- **Batch acknowledgment protocol needed.** 73 pairs x 5 agents = 365 individual acknowledgments. Batch acknowledgments ("Acknowledged Briefs #1-#21; concerns on #7") are practical.

## SOP Update Log

